from save_result import save

save('C0005','decorated-poisson-level-count-criterion','Conditional deep-level law from truncated marked-Poisson moments',
'''Fix alpha>0 and z>=0 finite. Let heights h_i form a Poisson process on R of intensity z*exp(-alpha*h) dh, carrying independent identically distributed measurable nonnegative marks K_i(s), s>=0, independent of the heights. Suppose exp(-alpha*s)*E K(s)->c in (0,infinity) and E K(s)^2<=C*exp(2*alpha*s) for every s>=0. Set S_t=sum_(h_i>=-t) K_i(t+h_i). Then S_t/(t*exp(alpha*t))->c*z in probability as t->infinity. The same conclusion holds with a finite almost-sure random intensity Z conditionally on Z and unconditionally, without assuming E Z<infinity. This is a conditional criterion for the limiting DGFF cluster process, not a proof of its moment hypotheses or the finite-N limit.''',
'''For a fixed upper cutoff H and t+H>=0 define S_t^H using -t<=h_i<=H. Poisson integration and the change of variable s=t+h give E S_t^H=z*exp(alpha*t)*integral_0^(t+H) exp(-alpha*s)*E K(s) ds. Cesaro averaging implies this divided by t*exp(alpha*t) tends to c*z. Its variance divided by t^2*exp(2*alpha*t) is at most z*C*exp(alpha*H)/(alpha*t^2), so the truncated variable converges in L2 for fixed H. The probability that the full and truncated sums differ is at most the probability of any height above H, namely 1-exp(-z*exp(-alpha*H)/alpha). First send t to infinity, then H to infinity, to prove convergence in probability. For random finite Z, these conditional error probabilities tend to zero and are bounded by one, so dominated convergence applies; E Z is not required. For finitely many disjoint spatial sets the same argument and a finite union bound give joint convergence with their respective intensity masses. Notice that E S_t is infinite whenever z>0 under the stated first-moment asymptotic: its integral extends over s in [0,infinity). Thus full-sum L2 convergence or uniform integrability must not be silently assumed. The earlier intake integral with exp(+alpha*s) inside reverses the change-of-variable sign.''',
['A marked Poisson process with the stated intensity and independent identically distributed decorations','alpha>0 and finite z (or a.s. finite random Z)','The two explicit first and second decoration moment hypotheses'],
'The moment hypotheses and the exchange or iterated use of the finite-N and deep-level limits are not verified for DGFF. The full limiting sum can have infinite expectation even while its normalized values converge in probability.')

save('C0065','susceptibility-derivative-inference-refuted','The recorded derivative inequality does not imply susceptibility divergence',
'''The implications theta(delta)~delta and theta'(delta)<=C*chi(delta), even with theta,chi positive and smooth on 0<delta<1, do not imply chi(delta)>=c/delta for any c>0. Therefore the earlier C0065 attempt's claimed inverse-distance lower bound does not follow from the two displayed premises. This refutes that inference, not the percolation lower bound itself.''',
'''Take theta(delta)=delta, chi(delta)=1 and C=1. Then theta'(delta)=1<=C*chi(delta), and theta(delta)/delta=1 exactly. However delta*chi(delta)=delta->0, so no positive c can satisfy chi(delta)>=c/delta for all sufficiently small delta. Both functions meet the stated smoothness and positivity requirements. Extra percolation inequalities could still prove a susceptibility lower bound, but they are additional premises and are not supplied by this derivative comparison.''',
['Only the two derivative-and-order premises quoted in the earlier attempt are being tested'],
'No claim is made that the actual high-dimensional finite-cluster susceptibility has this counterexample behavior. The claimed percolation theorem must be re-proved from valid percolation-specific inputs.')

save('C0065','binary-tree-finite-susceptibility-exponent','Exact supercritical finite susceptibility on the degree-three tree',
'''For Bernoulli bond percolation on the infinite degree-three tree, 1/2<p<1, write chi_f(p)=E_p[|C_o|*1_{|C_o|<infinity}]. Then chi_f(p)=((1-p)/p)^3*(2-p)/(2*p-1), and (p-1/2)*chi_f(p)->3/4 as p decreases to 1/2. Below criticality chi(p)=(1+p)/(1-2*p), with (1/2-p)*chi(p)->3/4. Thus the finite-susceptibility exponents agree and equal one on this tree.''',
'''Let t be the probability that a directed edge does not lead to infinity. Independence of the two forward branches gives t=1-p+p*t^2; the smaller root for p>1/2 is t=(1-p)/p. The root cluster is finite exactly when all three outward edge branches fail, an event of probability t^3. Conditional on such failure, each edge is open and has a finite forward subtree with probability r=p*t^2/t=p*t=1-p. The finite cluster therefore has root offspring Binomial(3,r), then Binomial(2,r) offspring thereafter. The mean total size conditional on finiteness is 1+3*r/(1-2*r)=(2-p)/(2*p-1). Multiplication by t^3 proves the formula and the limit. For p<1/2 all branches die, and the same offspring computation with r=p gives the subcritical formula.''',
['The infinite degree-three tree','Independent Bernoulli bond percolation','Susceptibility uses the unconditioned finite-cluster first moment'],
'This is a branching-tree special case. It proves neither the high-dimensional lattice result nor the spatial correlation-length exponents; graph distance on a tree has different geometry.')

save('C0090','finite-mean-scale-free-edge-decay','Finite weight means fix the annealed one-edge decay exponent',
'''Let iid positive weights W_x have finite mean mu, and conditionally independent edges have probability 1-exp(-lambda*W_x*W_y*|x-y|^(-b)), with b,lambda>0. Then the annealed one-edge probability is asymptotic to lambda*mu^2*|x-y|^(-b) as |x-y|->infinity. In particular for Pareto weights P(W>w)=w^(-rho), w>=1, rho>1, its coefficient is lambda*(rho/(rho-1))^2. A tail exponent b*rho cannot be inferred for this finite-mean regime from the weight-tail parameter.''',
'''Set V=W_x*W_y and s=lambda*|x-y|^(-b). For V>=0, 0<=(1-exp(-s*V))/s<=V, and the expression tends pointwise to V as s decreases to zero. Independence and the finite weight mean give E V=mu^2<infinity. Dominated convergence yields the asymptotic. For a Pareto variable the density rho*w^(-rho-1) on w>=1 gives E W=rho/(rho-1). This is an edge-probability calculation; it does not compute the connectedness two-point function or a critical exponent.''',
['Independent identically distributed positive weights with finite mean','The displayed conditional edge law','Fixed positive b and lambda'],
'The annealed one-edge probability is different from the annealed connectedness function. No mean-field boundary, critical dimension or continuity of theta is deduced.')

save('C0090','shared-weight-edge-covariance','Shared vertex weights strictly correlate annealed adjacent edges',
'''In the same scale-free edge model, take three distinct vertices 0,1,2, independent positive weights, and positive edge coefficients a,b. Let I_01 and I_02 be their edge indicators, conditionally independent given all weights. Then Cov(I_01,I_02)=Cov(g_a(W_0),g_b(W_0)), where g_a(w)=E[1-exp(-a*w*W_1)]. This covariance is positive when W_0 is nondegenerate. Thus an independent-edge model with the same edge marginals already disagrees on two-edge events.''',
'''Given W_0, integrating the independent endpoint weights leaves I_01 and I_02 independent Bernoulli variables with parameters g_a(W_0) and g_b(W_0), respectively. Conditional covariance is zero, so the covariance identity follows by conditioning. Each g is strictly increasing because the other weights are positive almost surely. For an independent copy W_0', covariance equals one half of E[(g_a(W_0)-g_a(W_0'))*(g_b(W_0)-g_b(W_0'))]. The integrand is nonnegative everywhere and strictly positive on {W_0!=W_0'}, which has positive probability for a nondegenerate distribution. All quantities are bounded and hence integrable, even for infinite-mean weights.''',
['Independent positive vertex weights','Conditional edge independence','Positive edge coefficients','Nondegenerate shared weight for strict positivity'],
'This invalidates direct substitution by independent edges at the level of joint laws. It does not rule out proving suitable stochastic comparisons or universality by a separate argument.')

save('C0143','regular-tree-infinite-contact-exponential-tail','Exponential infinite-cluster contact tail on every regular tree above criticality',
'''On the (b+1)-regular tree with b>=2, independent Bernoulli bond percolation at fixed p in (1/b,1), there are constants C,c>0 such that P_p(|C_o|<infinity and J_o>n)<=C*exp(-c*n), where J_o counts all graph edges from C_o to vertices in any infinite open cluster. The assertion also holds trivially at p=1.''',
'''Let t in (0,1) be the smaller solution of t=1-p+p*t^b. The root cluster is finite with probability t^(b+1). Conditional on finiteness, each outward edge opens into a finite branch with probability r=p*t^(b-1); this gives root offspring Binomial(b+1,r) and later offspring Binomial(b,r), independently. Strict convexity of f(x)=1-p+p*x^b-x, with roots t and 1, gives f'(t)<0, hence b*r<1. The forward progeny equation is F=z*(1-r+r*F)^b. The analytic implicit function theorem at (z,F)=(1,1), whose derivative in F is 1-b*r>0, gives a positive fixed point F_*(z)>1 for some z>1 sufficiently close to one. In fact F_*(z)>=z. The generating functions of generation-truncated progeny start at F_0(z)=z and satisfy F_(m+1)(z)=z*(1-r+r*F_m(z))^b. They increase and are bounded above by F_*(z), by induction. Monotone convergence then proves that the full progeny has a finite exponential moment at this z. The root-size generating function is z*(1-r+r*F(z))^(b+1), also finite. Markov's inequality gives an exponential tail for the finite cluster size. A finite connected tree with N vertices has exactly (b-1)*N+2 boundary edges by the degree sum, so J_o<=(b-1)*N+2. This transfers the size bound to J_o, with constants depending on b,p. At p=1 the finite-cluster event is empty.''',
['A regular tree of degree b+1, integer b>=2','Independent Bernoulli bond percolation','Fixed p>1/b','Contacts count graph edges, which must be closed at a finite open-cluster boundary'],
'This does not give the requested estimate on arbitrary transitive graphs. The constants deteriorate toward criticality; no uniform rate in p is asserted.')

save('P0019','holder-boundary-terminal-angle','Quantitative hemisphere containment at a Holder differentiable boundary',
'''Suppose a domain near a boundary point z is, in coordinates with outward unit normal e_d, contained in {x_d<g(x')}, with g(0)=0 and |g(x')|<=C*|x'|^(1+gamma), gamma>0. For any interior x approaching z=0, its normalized approach vector u=(z-x)/|z-x| satisfies e_d dot u>=-C*|z-x|^gamma. Consequently every limiting approach direction lies in the closed outward hemisphere. This applies pathwise to Brownian exits at such points.''',
'''Write r=|x|. Because x_d<=g(x')<=C*|x'|^(1+gamma)<=C*r^(1+gamma), the outward component of u=-x/r is -x_d/r>=-C*r^gamma. Let r decrease to zero along any convergent sequence of directions. The error vanishes, giving e_d dot u>=0. This is a deterministic local boundary statement, so continuity of a Brownian exit path is the only stochastic ingredient in this containment conclusion.''',
['A local boundary graph at the exit point with the displayed remainder bound','The orientation e_d is outward','Interior points approach that boundary point'],
'Containment does not establish that every hemisphere direction occurs. Arbitrary nonsmooth domains need not have the displayed boundary graph.')

save('P0019','halfspace-terminal-hemisphere-sketch','Half-space case via reversal and logarithmic-time Gaussian recurrence',
'''PROOF SKETCH, pending independent verification: Brownian motion started inside a half-space in dimension d>=2 should have the whole closed outward hemisphere as its cluster set of normalized approach directions at its first boundary hit. The proposed route uses the conditional reversed normal path as a three-dimensional Bessel bridge, the reversed tangential paths as Brownian bridges, and dense Gaussian directions in logarithmic time.''',
'''Condition on exit time T=t>0 and tangential exit location. Reversing the terminal path is expected to give an independent product of (d-1) Brownian bridges and a BES(3) bridge, started at zero, on the reversed interval. On any [0,epsilon] with epsilon<t, bridge laws are absolutely continuous with respect to unconditioned Brownian motion and BES(3); the density is the remaining-time transition density divided by its starting value. Represent BES(3) as the radius of three independent Brownian coordinates, independently of the d-1 tangential coordinates. For a Brownian vector W of dimension d+2, V_u=exp(u/2)*W_(exp(-u)) has stationary centered Gaussian covariance exp(-|u-v|/2), and hence its Gaussian stationary process is ergodic. Every open cone of positive Gaussian mass is visited infinitely often. The map v -> (v_1,...,v_(d-1), sqrt(v_d^2+v_(d+1)^2+v_(d+2)^2))/|v| has support the closed outward hemisphere. A countable base of cones gives all directions as subsequential limits. Absolute continuity would pass this almost sure property to the reversed bridges. The unresolved proof-check obligation is to derive the stated conditional reversal and bridge density from killed one-dimensional Brownian transition and first-hitting densities, including the zero starting boundary. This obligation is not replaced by a simulation.''',
['Half-space domain','Brownian motion starting at positive normal height','The conditional reversal and bridge absolute-continuity identities still need a full check'],
'Only a sketch is claimed; it is a concept card, not an accepted theorem. It does not cover arbitrary domains in d>2.',label='sketch',kind='concept')

save('P0048','ergodic-forests-arbitrary-finite-component-count','Invariant ergodic spanning forests can have every finite component count',
'''For each integer k>=2, the Cayley graph G_k of Z with generators {+1,-1,+k,-k} has a deterministic translation-invariant spanning forest with exactly k components. Keep exactly the length-k edges. Its point-mass law is ergodic under translations. Thus translation invariance and ergodicity alone cannot exclude any finite component count greater than one.''',
'''Each retained component is one residue class modulo k, with edges between consecutive elements of that class. It is a bi-infinite path and hence has no cycle. Every integer belongs to exactly one of these k residue classes, so the forest spans G_k and has k components. Translation by one permutes the classes and preserves the whole edge set, proving invariance. A point-mass probability measure assigns every measurable event probability zero or one, and is therefore ergodic. The earlier k=2 example is its first instance. None of these deterministic forests is asserted to be a free uniform spanning forest.''',
['k is an integer at least two','G_k has both length-one and length-k edges','Only length-k edges are retained'],
'This refutes a proposed inference from ergodicity, not the one-or-infinity classification for the actual FUSF law. It adds no nonunimodular FUSF counterexample.')
